WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.235
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.235 --> 00:00:01.860
YUFENG GUO: Hi.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.120
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.210
And today we're going to explore
how Google Cloud's Data Lake

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.370
solution allows
enterprises to securely

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.860
ingest, store,
process, and analyze

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.490
any volume or type of data.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.309
For example, a financial
services company

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.290
might be getting an influx
of loan applications.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.040
It's important to accelerate
the time to process and approve

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.930
the loans using machine
learning in order

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:28.190
to get the money to the right
businesses and people faster.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.290
Let's walk through how
a data engineer and data

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.659
scientist can work together
on the solution using

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:36.610
Cloud Storage and Dataproc.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.550
Dataproc is a fully managed
service allowing enterprises

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.560
to run Apache Spark
and Apache Hadoop

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.750
clusters with
30-plus open source

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.110
components in a simple,
secure, and cost-efficient way.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.630
In the first step
of the pipeline,

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.920
the data engineer will
run Spark jobs on Dataproc

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.518
to convert the loan data from
CSV tables into Hive tables.

00:00:56.518 --> 00:00:58.060
The metadata for
the Hive tables will

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.000
be saved in a new fully managed
Dataproc Metastore service.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.600
This allows for easy discovery
and governance of your data.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.200
You can now easily create a
Dataproc Metastore service

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.230
from the Dataproc UI and
start using it across clusters

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.000
by sharing the service endpoint.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.960
In Spark, it requires just
three additional lines of code

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.310
to connect to Dataproc Metastore
and enable Hive table support.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.500
To run the Spark
job, we now support

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.370
running Dataproc on
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.260
It's simple to set
up by just including

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.000
one additional line
of code to create

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.970
a Dataproc on GKE cluster.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.720
Spark jobs will now run on
Kubernetes instead of YARN.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.910
This reduces code
dependencies and allows

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.750
you to better utilize idle
cloud or on prem resources.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.850
The data engineer can
then run the Spark job

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.070
on the Google Kubernetes
engine cluster

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:51.310
to convert the CSV
data to Hive tables.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.070
And data scientists can
now begin their analysis

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.930
of the loan data
in the Hive table

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.487
by connecting to the shared
Dataproc Metastore service.

00:01:59.487 --> 00:02:01.570
To create the machine
learning features and model,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.840
we'll use Jupyter notebooks,
a popular data science tool.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.090
We've made it easy to use
Jupyter notebooks and Apache

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.300
Spark together
with Dataproc hub.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.880
NVIDIA GPUs can be added
to the Dataproc cluster

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.770
along with installing
of the rapids, which

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.500
includes XGBoost to be
used in Jupyter notebooks

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.685
to create the model.

00:02:18.685 --> 00:02:21.790
And using GPUs instead of
CPUs can improve training time

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.490
by over 40x.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.775
Let's see how you can
use these features.

00:02:26.775 --> 00:02:28.150
Start from the
Dataproc notebooks

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.910
UI where you can now create a
Dataproc hub instance, which

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.890
makes it easier to
use and manage Jupyter

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.930
notebooks for large teams.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.780
Admins can manage and control
what cluster configurations can

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.290
be used by users, allowing
for improved resource and cost

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.580
management.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.260
You can then use
a PySpark notebook

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.300
to create your XGBoost model.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.800
First, read the data using GPUs.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.500
Next, train a
classification model

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.070
to predict loan approvals,
also using GPUs.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.830
Then evaluate the model
with 1 being accepted

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.905
and 0 being denied.

00:02:58.905 --> 00:03:01.030
The model can then be saved
to Google Cloud Storage

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.512
and is easily loaded to be
used again in other Spark jobs.

00:03:05.512 --> 00:03:07.470
The model is now ready
to be used in production

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.350
to do batch loan approvals
on new data lending and Hive

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.988
tables.

00:03:12.988 --> 00:03:15.280
In this demo, we showed how
a financial service company

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.700
can create a model to accelerate
loan processing time using

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.340
Spark on Dataproc running
on Kubernetes, Jupyter

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.890
notebooks, and NVIDIA
Rapids and GPUs,

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:28.238
all while storing the Hive
metadata in Dataproc Metastore.

00:03:28.238 --> 00:03:30.280
Head on over to these
links to learn more and get

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.380
started with building your data
lake on Google Cloud today.

